2026-08-25T00:00:07Z (RFD66606-2026) 'Context and problem statement
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD2026  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:08Z (RFD66606-2026) 'A commit subject is the first line a reader meets in `git log`, a blame, or a release note. Two conventions compete for how it reads. Conventional Commits prefixes each subject with a machine-readable type and optional scope, such as `feat:` or `fix(parser):`, and lower-cases the summary that follows. Plain prose writes the subject as an ordinary capitalised sentence. How should a commit subject in this repo read?
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2026  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:09Z (RFD66606-2026) 'Decision drivers
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD2026  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:10Z (RFD66606-2026) '- A reader scans the subject as a sentence first, and the meaning sits at the front rather than after a colon.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2026  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:11Z (RFD66606-2026) '- The repos here run no tooling that consumes a commit type: no semantic-release, no changelog keyed on `feat` or `fix`.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2026  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:12Z (RFD66606-2026) '- One rule covers every commit, so review needs no judgement about which type applies.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2026  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:13Z (RFD66606-2026) 'Considered options
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD2026  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:14Z (RFD66606-2026) '- Conventional Commits, with a `type(scope):` prefix on every subject.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2026  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:15Z (RFD66606-2026) '- Sentence-case prose subjects with no prefix.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2026  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:16Z (RFD66606-2026) '- Free choice of style per author.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2026  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:17Z (RFD66606-2026) 'Decision outcome
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD2026  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:18Z (RFD66606-2026) 'Chosen option: sentence-case prose with no prefix, because the subject stays a sentence a reader understands on sight, and the repos gain nothing from a commit type that no tool reads.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2026  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:19Z (RFD66606-2026) 'A commit subject opens with a capital letter and reads as a plain sentence, such as `Add the macOS and Windows release workflows`. It carries no `feat:`, `fix:`, `chore:`, or `type(scope):` prefix, and no trailing period. The body, where present, states what the change makes true of the system and why.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2026  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:20Z (RFD66606-2026) 'Consequences
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD2026  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:21Z (RFD66606-2026) '- Good, because the subject reads as a summary on its own, with nothing to strip before the meaning.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2026  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:22Z (RFD66606-2026) '- Good, because the rule holds for every commit, so no author weighs whether a change counts as a `feat` or a `fix`.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2026  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:23Z (RFD66606-2026) '- Bad, because a changelog tool that groups commits by type finds no signal here, so adopting one later needs a different marker or a history rewrite.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2026  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:24Z (RFD66606-2026) 'Scope
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD2026  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:25Z (RFD66606-2026) 'The rule holds on every repository this workspace commits to, forks included. A fix to a fork lands in our fork and nothing is sent upstream (RFD66606-2294, "Where a session posts"), so a fork's history past the upstream commit is ours and reads like the rest. A fork of a Conventional-Commits upstream carries sentence-case subjects on top of the upstream's prefixed ones. A branch that merges the upstream passes the upstream ref to the gate with `--exclude`, so only our commits are read.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2026  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:26Z (RFD66606-2026) 'Confirmation
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD2026  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:27Z (RFD66606-2026) 'The rule is machine-checked by the `practices` hook (`scripts/check_practices.exs`, RFD66606-2294), on push and in CI in every repository that takes the hook, and by `scripts/check_commit_style.py` on its own. Each reads the commits reachable from `HEAD` and from neither `<base>` nor any `--exclude` ref, for three properties:
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2026  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:28Z (RFD66606-2026) '1. No Conventional-Commits `type:` or `type(scope):` prefix on the subject.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2026  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:29Z (RFD66606-2026) '2. Subject opens with an uppercase letter, digit, or bracket.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2026  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:30Z (RFD66606-2026) '3. Subject does not end with a trailing period.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2026  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:31Z (RFD66606-2026) 'Neither gate reads a remote, so a fork is held to the same three properties. The Python gate's self-test carries six subject controls (three that pass, three that fail), four that run the script itself on a prefixed and a sentence-case commit in a scratch repository, once behind a V-Sekai-fire remote and once behind a fork remote, and two on a merged upstream commit with a prefixed subject, which fails when read and passes once its ref is excluded. The practices self-test carries the same rules in both directions.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2026  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:32Z (RFD66606-2026) '    python scripts/check_commit_style.py --base origin/main
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2026  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:33Z (RFD66606-2026) '    python scripts/check_commit_style.py --base origin/main --exclude upstream/master
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2026  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:34Z (RFD66606-2026) '    python scripts/check_commit_style.py --self-test
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2026  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:35Z (RFD66606-2026) 'Review reads each subject as a capitalised sentence with no type prefix and no trailing period. The history after this decision shows subjects in that form.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2026  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:36Z (RFD66606-2026) 'More information
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD2026  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:37Z (RFD66606-2026) 'This pairs with the tenseless continuous-present voice (`rfd/2025-tenseless-continuous-present-voice`): a commit body states what the change makes true of the system, the same way comments and docs do.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2026  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:38Z (RFD66606-2026) 'The problem
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD2026  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:39Z (RFD66606-2026) 'Conventional Commits prefixes each subject with a machine-readable type and optional scope, such as `feat:` or `fix(parser):`, and lower-cases the summary that follows. Plain prose writes the subject as an ordinary capitalised sentence. How should a commit subject in this repo read?
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2026  1 LINE
 Text:Written
